to add a remote origin: git remote add origin git@github.com:your-username/your-repo.git
To update an existing remote to use SSH: git remote set-url origin git@github.com:your-username/your-repo.git

download changes from a remote repo: git fetch

git pull: merge ur local repo with remote repo branch

To delete a remote branch:git push origin --delete branch-name

rename branch: git branch -m old-name new-name


pull branch from github: first see allnbranches using git branch -a

then checkout in the branch u wanna pull: git checkout <branchtobepulled>

pushing branch to github: git push origin <branchnametobepushed>


create a folder:   mkdir <foldername>
create a file : touch <filename>
see the status: git status
creating a new branch (this just creates a pointer to the new branch) : git branch <branchname>
seperate it from main branch: git checkout <branchname>
switching branch : git check <to swutch branch>
delete branch: git branch -d <branchtodelete>
to see what is inside the file: cat <filename>
unstage file : git restore --staged <filename>
Wipes out all local changes you made to that file since your last commit: git restore <filename>
To view the history: git log
can also delete changes using: git reset <commit_id>   (will get id from git log)
Stage all changes: git add --all or git add -A
Commit all tracked changes (skip staging): git commit -a -m "message"
Write Multi-line Commit Messages: git commit (no -m)

tags: tag is like a pointer to the current commit
light weight tag: git tag v1.0
annotated tag: git tag -a v1.0 -m "Version 1.0 release"

to tag a specific commit:
git tag v1.1 1a2b3c4d

see all tags: git tag, show tag details: git show v1.0
#PUSHING TAGS TO REMOTE: push one tag: git push origin v1.0
                         push all tags: git push --tags
#deleting tags:git tag -d v1.0
delete tag from remote repo: git push origin --delete tag v1.0

move a tag to a different commit and update the remote:  git tag -f v1.0 <new-commit-hash>
                                                         git push --force origin v1.0
                                                      
Save your current changes (both staged and unstaged tracked files) with: git stash
							git stash push -m "WIP: homepage redesign"
							
what is different between your working directory and the last commit : git diff
							
compare two commits: git diff <commit1> <commit2>

hat is different between your staged files and the last commit:  git diff --staged
									 


